{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.9,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R36"],
      "pins": ["R36.1", "R36.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTP", "VOUTN"],
      "observed": "R36 is specified with value '24k' but its description says '24Ω' and its MPN 'FRC0805J240TSD' is a 24Ω 0805 resistor (LCSC C2930261).",
      "expected": "R36 is a PoE detection resistor across VOUTP/VOUTN and should be 24kΩ, matching the value field, not 24Ω.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(comp (ref \"R36\") (value \"24k\") ... (description \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\") ... (field (name \"MPN\") \"FRC0805J240TSD\")"}
      ],
      "impact": "If the BOM is followed, a 24Ω resistor is installed instead of 24kΩ, shorting the PoE input and preventing PoE detection; it may also overheat and damage the board.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["R60", "D30"],
      "pins": ["R60.2", "D30.2", "D30.4"],
      "nets": ["ESTOP_OUT", "Net-(D30-K2)"],
      "observed": "R60 pin 2 is connected to ESTOP_OUT, while D30 pin 4 (A2) is also on ESTOP_OUT. D30 pin 2 (K2) connects via R60 to the same net ESTOP_OUT, so the second LED of D30 has its anode and cathode both tied to ESTOP_OUT through R60.",
      "expected": "R60 should connect D30 K2 to GND, so that LED2 lights when ESTOP_OUT is high.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"67\") (name \"ESTOP_OUT\") ... (node (ref \"D30\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"A2\")) ... (node (ref \"R60\") (pin \"2\")))"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"129\") (name \"Net-(D30-K2)\") (node (ref \"D30\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"K2\")) (node (ref \"R60\") (pin \"1\")))"}
      ],
      "impact": "D30's e-stop indicator LED (LED2) can never illuminate because both its anode and cathode return to ESTOP_OUT. The board loses an e-stop status indication.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "category": "short_to_ground",
      "components": ["U10", "U11"],
      "pins": ["U10.5", "U11.5"],
      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "The isolated-side grounds of both isolated CAN transceivers (U10 GND2 pin 5 and U11 GND2 pin 5) are tied directly to the main board GND net.",
      "expected": "For isolated CAN buses, GND2 should be an isolated ground separate from the logic-side GND1, and VCC2 should come from an isolated supply. The specification states the board implements 'two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1) ... through an isolated CAN transceiver (U11/companion)'.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9) through an isolated CAN transceiver (U11/companion)"},
        {"source": "netlist", "quote": "(net (code \"80\") (name \"GND\") ... (node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\")) ... (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\"))"}
      ],
      "impact": "The galvanic isolation between the CAN buses and the motherboard is defeated, removing the intended ground-loop and transient protection and potentially allowing damaging currents between separate grounds.",
      "confidence": 0.85
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design contains multiple anomalies: R36 is specified as 24kΩ but the BOM part is 24Ω; R60 is miswired so D30's second LED never lights; and the isolated CAN transceivers have their isolated grounds tied to board GND, defeating the intended isolation."
}

